## Terminology ## {#terminology}

- <dfn>Dataset</dfn> refers to the definition given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:Dataset`.
- <dfn>Distribution</dfn> refers to the definition given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:Distribution`.
- <dfn>Data Service</dfn> refers to the definitions given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:DataService`.
- <dfn>Resource</dfn> refers to the generic term encompassing [=datasets=], [=distributions=] and [=data services=], defined in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] as `dcat:Resource`
- <dfn>Catalog</dfn> refers to a [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] resource of type `dcat:Catalog`.
- <dfn>Shape</dfn> refers to a [[!SHACL]] `sh:NodeShape` that formally describes constraints over RDF data.
- <dfn>Discovery</dfn> refers to the process specified in [[#logical-flow]] for identifying relevant RDF [=resources=] over one or more [=catalogs=].
- <dfn>Client</dfn> refers to a client application that implements the [[#algorithm]] and supports the [[#logical-flow]].

# Describing DCAT(-AP) resource content with shapes # {#dcat-shapes}

This section defines a set of alternative ways in which DCAT(-AP) [=resources=], 
specifically referring to RDF [=datasets=] or [=data services=],
can be extended to include references to data [=shapes=] that semantically describe their content.  
The extensions are designed to be minimal and compatible with existing DCAT-AP profiles.

Note: The [[#logical-flow]] and related [[#algorithm]] are designed to support all the following data model extensions.
In the presence of more than one alternative, a [=discovery=] [=client=] will not prioritise any of them and should treat them as a logical OR relation.

## Relating to shapes via `dcterms:conformsTo` ## {#conforms-to}

In this approach we establish the use of the existing property `dcterms:conformsTo` [[!DCTERMS]], 
as described by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] and [[!DCAT-AP]], to link an RDF [=resource=] defined as part of a [=catalog=],
to a [=shape=] that describes its content.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the use of `dcterms:conformsTo` is informally suggested for linking a resource to a relevant standard,
that specifies its model, **schema**, ontology, application profile, among others.
SHACL shapes may be considered as a type of schema, as they define the structure and constraints that RDF instance data must adhere to.

No further clarifications are provided in [[!DCAT-AP]] regarding the expected use of `dcterms:conformsTo`,
beyond the informal suggestion to link to relevant standards, understood as instances of `dcterms:Standard`.

**Figure 1** shows how any kind of RDF [=resource=] that has been declared in a [=catalog=], may be linked to a related [=shape=] via `dcterms:conformsTo`.

<figure>
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `dcterms:conformsTo`. 
    </figcaption>
    <object type='image/svg+xml' data='img/figure-1.svg' width='700'></object>
</figure>

**Example 1** shows corresponding RDF representations for a [=dataset=], [=distribution=], and [=data service=], respectively linked to their [=shapes=].

<div class="example" id="example-1">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(1, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(1, 1)">Distribution Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(1, 2)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Distribution` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:distribution ex:myDistribution.
        
    ex:myDistribution a dcat:Distribution;
        dcat:downloadURL <https://example.org/dataset.ttl>;
        dcat:mediaType <https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle>;
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:servesDataset ex:myDataset;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape, ex:myOtherShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].

    ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:integer;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
</div>

Note: A related [=shape=] to a [=data service=] could be referring to the constraints of either the input or output data (or both) of such service.
Via the `dcterms:conformsTo` property, no distinction can be made about the role of a related shape 
(see [[#qualified-relation]] for an alternative where it is possible). 
However, linking multiple shapes to a data service should not entail any conflicts for the [=discovery=] process, 
given its inclusive nature based on a logical OR operation. 

## Relating to shapes via `dcat:qualifiedRelation` ## {#qualified-relation}

In this approach we establish the use of the property `dcat:qualifiedRelation` as described by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] and [[!DCAT-AP]], 
to indirectly link an RDF [=resource=] defined as part of a [=catalog=], to a [=shape=] that describes its content or the expected structure of input/output data for [=data services=].
This approach follows an n-ary [[SWBP-N-ARYRELATIONS]] or qualified [[LDPATTERNS]] data modelling pattern.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the use of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is informally specified as a way to define semantically richer relations
among resources. This is accomplished with the use of the `dcat:Relationship` class, on which specific roles can be set,
to further describe the nature of the relationship between a resource and another asset.
For specifying types of roles, the use of controlled vocabularies is encouraged, which is reflected by the existing subclass relation
between the `dcat:Role` class and `skos:Concept`.

The domain of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is `dcat:Resource`, which includes DCAT [=datasets=] and [=data services=] but not [=distributions=].

No further guidelines nor usage notes are given for `dcat:qualifiedRelation` and `dcat:Relationship` in [[!DCAT-AP]].

**Figure 2** shows how a shape can be linked to any type of resource using the `dcat:qualifiedRelation` property.

<figure>
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `dcat:qualifiedRelation`. 
    </figcaption>
    <object type='image/svg+xml' data='img/figure-2.svg' width='700'></object>
</figure>

In practice, for the [=discovery=] process, a [=client=] needs to traverse the property path `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`,
in search for an associated [=shape=]. This specification does not mandate a specific role concept value via `dcat:hadRole`.
A discovery client must assess all existing qualified relations (see [[#logical-flow]]).

Note: [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] suggests the use of coded term lists such as [[IANA-RELATIONS]], or [[ISO-19115-1]],
to specify a `dcat:Relationship` role via the `dcat:hadRole` property.
However no established standard vocabulary currently exists for defining a role type that specifically conveys a relationship between a data resource and an associated [=shape=].
In section [[#roles]], we discuss how a discovery client may handle qualified relations to identify associated shapes.

**Example 2** shows corresponding RDF representations for a dataset and data service, respectively linked to their shapes via `dcat:qualifiedRelation`.
According to [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the domain of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is `dcat:Resource`, which excludes `dcat:Distribution` instances.

<div class="example" id="example-2">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(2, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(2, 1)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:qualifiedRelation [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole <http://www.iana.org/assignments/relation/describedby>; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myShape
        ].

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        dcat:qualifiedRelation [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole disc:InputShape; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myShape
        ], [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole disc:OutputShape; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myOtherShape
        ].

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].

    ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:integer;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
</div>

Note: The qualified relation pattern allows for a more granular description of the type or relation
that a given shape has with a resource. For example, as it is shown in **Example 2 (Data Service Example)**,
where the qualified relations describe the shapes of both input and output data of a data service independently.
Roles may only influence the discovery process when defined as indicated in section [[#roles]].

## Relating to shapes via `sh:shapesGraph` ## {#shapes-graph}

In this approach we establish the use of the property `sh:shapesGraph`, as defined  by [[!SHACL]],
to link an RDF resource listed as part of a [=catalog=], to a shape that describes its content.

The `sh:shapesGraph` property is used in [[!SHACL]] to indicate a graph where the validation shapes are defined.
From the perspective of DCAT, the use of `sh:shapesGraph` constitutes a model extension 
that is not currently contemplated by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] nor [[!DCAT-AP]].

Note: An example of such an extension can be observed in the work of Franck Michel et al. [[WEB-API-DISC]],
where they propose the use of `sh:shapesGraph` to link a `dcat:Distribution` to a graph containing its shape definitions.

**Figure 3** shows how any kind of RDF resource that has been declared in a catalog, may be linked to a related shape via `sh:shapesGraph`.

<figure>
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `sh:shapesGraph`. 
    </figcaption>
    <object type='image/svg+xml' data='img/figure-3.svg' width='700'></object>
</figure>

An associated shapes graph linked via `sh:shapesGraph` may contain one or more shape definitions.
It is assumed that these must be related to the resource that links to them. 

**Example 3** shows corresponding RDF representations for a dataset, distribution, and data service, 
respectively linked to their shapes via `sh:shapesGraph`.

<div class="example" id="example-3">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(3, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(3, 1)">Distribution Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(3, 2)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:Distribution` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:distribution ex:myDistribution.
        
    ex:myDistribution a dcat:Distribution;
        dcat:downloadURL <https://example.org/dataset.ttl>;
        dcat:mediaType <https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle>;
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`  
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:servesDataset ex:myDataset;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].

        ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:integer;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
</div>

# Shape relationship roles # {#roles}

Shape relationship roles provide guidance to a [=discovery=] [=client=] about whether comparing two [=shapes=]
is meaningful. They are applicable only to shape descriptions expressed through `dcat:qualifiedRelation`
([[#qualified-relation]]), where the role is the value of `dcat:hadRole` on the corresponding `dcat:Relationship`.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], `dcat:Role` is a subclass of `skos:Concept` and the value of `dcat:hadRole` is a role *instance*, not a
class. Accordingly, this specification defines the roles below as concepts of a <dfn export>shape role scheme</dfn>, identified by
`disc:ShapeRoles`, rather than as subclasses of `dcat:Role`:

- <dfn id="InputShape" export><code>disc:InputShape</code></dfn> identifies a [=shape=] that describes data accepted as input by a [=data service=].
- <dfn id="OutputShape" export><code>disc:OutputShape</code></dfn> identifies a [=shape=] that describes data produced as output by a [=data service=].

The following RDF statements formally express these definitions:

```turtle
@prefix dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>.
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>.
@prefix disc: <https://dishacled.github.io/discovery-specification/#>.
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>.

disc:ShapeRoles
	a skos:ConceptScheme ;
	dcterms:title "Shape relationship roles"@en ;
	skos:hasTopConcept disc:InputShape, disc:OutputShape .

disc:InputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "Input shape role"@en ;
	skos:definition "A role identifying a data shape that describes data accepted as input by a data service."@en ;
	skos:topConceptOf disc:ShapeRoles ;
	skos:inScheme disc:ShapeRoles .

disc:OutputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "Output shape role"@en ;
	skos:definition "A role identifying a data shape that describes data produced as output by a data service."@en ;
	skos:topConceptOf disc:ShapeRoles ;
	skos:inScheme disc:ShapeRoles .
```

The two concepts are distinct: a single `dcat:Relationship` must not carry both `disc:InputShape` and `disc:OutputShape` as
values of `dcat:hadRole`. A [=client=] that encounters such a relationship must treat its role as undetermined and assess the
shape without role-based filtering.

When a user assigns one of these roles to the shape supplied in a discovery request (see [[#starting-from]]), a [=client=] should use
the complementary role to select the shapes of a candidate [=data service=] for relevance assessment:

- a user-provided shape with role `disc:InputShape` should be assessed against candidate shapes with role `disc:OutputShape`; and
- a user-provided shape with role `disc:OutputShape` should be assessed against candidate shapes with role `disc:InputShape`.

Discovery clients following this specification should also support application-specific roles declared as narrower concepts of
`disc:InputShape` or `disc:OutputShape` through `skos:broader`. Such a role must be treated according to the corresponding
broader role when selecting complementary candidate shapes. A role that is neither of these concepts nor transitively narrower
than one of them has no matching semantics defined by this specification and should not be used to exclude an otherwise
eligible shape.

<div class=example>
An application-specific role that refines `disc:OutputShape`, and which a [=client=] must therefore treat as an output role.
```turtle
ex:GeoJSONOutputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "GeoJSON output shape role"@en ;
	skos:broader disc:OutputShape .
```
</div>

If the user does not indicate a role for the provided shape, or a candidate shape has no `dcat:hadRole`, the [=client=]
must not exclude the candidate based on role. In that case, relevance is assessed without role-based filtering as specified
in [[#resource-relevance]].


# Discovery logical flow # {#logical-flow}

This section describes the logical steps that a client needs to follow to perform a discovery process.
An overview of the logical flow is shown in **Figure 4**.

<figure>
    <figcaption>
        Overview of a discovery process's logical sequence. 
    </figcaption>
    <object type='image/svg+xml' data='img/figure-4.svg' width='800'></object>
</figure>

In the next subsections a detailed description of each step is provided.

## Discovery request ## {#starting-from}

This is the initial step (labeled as 1️⃣ in **Figure 4**). A client starts a discovery process when it receives as input:
1. a set of formal constraints, expressed either as an <dfn>input SHACL shape</dfn> (in any RDF serialization ([string](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#javascript-string)))
    or an <dfn>input SPARQL query</dfn> ([string](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#javascript-string)); and
2. optionally, a <dfn>shape role</dfn> value for an [=input SHACL shape=], as defined in [[#roles]]; and
3. optionally, a set of one or more <dfn>catalog URL</dfn>s ([IRI](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2)),
    which must point to a dereferenceable RDF representation of a catalog. Alternatively, a client may operate over previously retrieved catalogs.

Note: A [=shape role=] only makes sense when defined together with an [=input SHACL shape=].
It should be ignored if present together with an [=input SPARQL query=].

## Catalog retrieval ## {#catalog-retrieval}

This step (labeled as 2️⃣ in **Figure 4**) involves retrieving the RDF representation of each catalog provided as input.
A client must perform an HTTP GET request to each [=catalog URL=],
which must be dereferenceable to any RDF serialization (e.g. [[TURTLE]], [[RDF11-XML]], [[JSON-LD]], etc.).

Note: Even though in **Figure 4** the catalog retrieval step is shown as a sequential process,
a client may choose to perform the retrieval of multiple catalogs in parallel,
or to locally cache the retrieved catalogs,
to optimise the overall performance of the discovery process.

## Extraction of associated shapes ## {#extract-shapes}

This step (labeled as 3️⃣ in **Figure 4**) involves parsing the RDF representation of each retrieved [=catalog=],
to extract all the shapes associated with RDF resources declared in it.
A client must identify all resources of type `dcat:Dataset`, `dcat:Distribution`, and `dcat:DataService`,
and for each resource, extract any associated shape(s) using the three approaches defined in section [[#dcat-shapes]].
Given the mutually inclusive relation of these approaches, a client must consider all of them when extracting shapes.
That is, for every resource a client must:
1. follow the property path `dcterms:conformsTo` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances ([[#conforms-to]]).
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>

        SELECT ?resource ?shape WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:Distribution dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                dcterms:conformsTo ?shape.

            ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
        }
        ```
    </div>
2. follow the property path `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances ([[#qualified-relation]]).
    For each extracted shape, the client must also retain any provided [=shape role=] and assess it against the value of `dcat:hadRole` properties according to the rules defined in section [[#roles]].
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>

        SELECT ?resource ?shape ?role WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                dcat:qualifiedRelation ?relation.

            ?relation dcterms:relation ?shape.
            OPTIONAL { ?relation dcat:hadRole ?role. }

            ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
        }
        ```
    </div>
3. follow the property path `sh:shapesGraph` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances within the referenced graph ([[#shapes-graph]]).
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>
        
        SELECT ?resource ?shape WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:Distribution dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                sh:shapesGraph ?shapesGraph.

            GRAPH ?shapesGraph {
                ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
            }
        }
        ```
    </div>

The equivalent SPARQL queries listed above, make the assumption that all RDF quads for both resources and associated shapes,
are locally available as part of a catalog RDF document. However, it may be the case that a related shape is linked as a remote resource.
In such case, **a client must also dereference the shape's** [**IRI**](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2) to retrieve its RDF representation.

## Relevance assessment ## {#resource-relevance}

This step (labeled as 4️⃣ in **Figure 4**) determines how relevant each resource is,
based on a containment assessment among their extracted shapes and the given [=input SHACL shape=] or [=input SPARQL query=] 
expressing the client's formal constraints.

A client must evaluate the containment relationship ([[#discovery-result]]) of the shapes of each candidate [=resource=],
taken together, against the [=input SHACL shape=] or [=input SPARQL query=] constraints,
by applying the [=query-shape subsumption algorithm=] described in section [[#algorithm]].
The algorithm is applied once per candidate resource and yields one [=discovery result=] for it, not one per shape.

Role-based filtering ([[#roles]]) selects which of a resource's shapes may determine that result, and does not remove the
others from the assessment. A client must therefore partition the shapes it extracted for a candidate resource into:

- the <dfn>deciding shapes</dfn>: the role-compliant shapes, or all of the resource's shapes when no role applies. Only these
    contribute to the classification of a star pattern in [[#algorithm-steps]]; and
- the <dfn>available shapes</dfn>: every shape extracted for the resource, including the deciding ones. These remain
    resolvable as targets of `sh:node` references, so that a [=star-pattern dependency=] can be followed even when the
    referenced shape is not itself eligible to decide the result.

A client must not discard a non-deciding shape before running the algorithm: doing so would break the resolution of
[=star-pattern dependencies=] and can lower an otherwise [=contained=] result.

## Presentation of results ## {#return-results}

In this last step, (labeled as 5️⃣ in **Figure 4**) the client returns an ordered (sub-)set of resource identifiers ([IRI](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2))
from the considered data catalogs, labeled according to their relevance degree as specified in [[#discovery-result]], with respect to the given input constraints ([=input SHACL shape=] / [=input SPARQL query=]).

# Algorithm specification # {#algorithm}

This section defines a <dfn>query-shape subsumption algorithm</dfn> that aims to determine the <i>relevance</i> of an RDF resource,
described by a set of associated SHACL shapes, towards a given set of formal constraints, 
expressed in the form of a SHACL shape or a SPARQL query.
The algorithm decomposes the given input constraints into [=star patterns=] and compares each pattern with the
constraints expressed by the resource shapes. The result is expressed in the form of a qualified degree 
that indicates whether the resource shapes contain, partially align with, or reject the input constraints.

First, a set of preliminary concepts are defined, followed by a description of the logical steps taken by the algorithm.

## Preliminaries ## {#algorithm-preliminaries}

Let <math><mi>I</mi></math>, <math><mi>B</mi></math>, <math><mi>L</mi></math>, and <math><mi>V</mi></math> denote the mutually disjoint
sets of IRIs, blank nodes, literals, and variables, respectively. The algorithm is specified using the following concepts,
adapted from the formalization of query-shape subsumption in [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]]:

- <dfn>triple pattern</dfn>: a tuple <math><mi>tp</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub></mfenced></math> in
    <math><mfenced><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mfenced><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mfenced><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></mfenced></math>.
    <math><mi>S</mi><mfenced><mi>tp</mi></mfenced></math> and <math><mi>O</mi><mfenced><mi>tp</mi></mfenced></math> denote its subject and object;
    <math><mi>O</mi></math> is undefined when the object is a literal.
- <dfn>star pattern</dfn> [[STAR-PATTERNS]]: for a query <math><mi>Q</mi></math> and
    <math><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></math>,
    <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>tp</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mfenced><mi>tp</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>.
    In other words, a star pattern is the set of [=triple patterns=] of <math><mi>Q</mi></math> sharing subject <math><mi>x</mi></math>.
    <div class=example>
    Example of a [=star pattern=] defined as part of a SPARQL query.
    ```sparql
    SELECT * WHERE {
        ?subject a ex:Entity;  #--|
            ex:pred1 ?obj1;    #  | start pattern 
            ex:pred2 ?obj2.    #--|
    }
    ```
    </div>
- <dfn export>graph star pattern</dfn>: the least set containing a designated root [=star pattern=] and, recursively, every star pattern
    whose subject is the non-literal object of a triple pattern in a star pattern already in the set. An edge from a triple
    pattern to such a star pattern is a <dfn>star-pattern dependency</dfn>. This transitive definition includes dependencies at
    arbitrary depth, not only direct children of the root.
    <div class=example>
    Example of a [=star pattern=] defined as part of a SPARQL query.
    ```sparql
    SELECT * WHERE {
        ?subject a ex:Entity;  #--|-------------------|
            ex:pred1 ?obj1;    #  | root star pattern |
            ex:pred2 ?obj2.    #--|                   |
                               #                      |
        ?obj1 a ex:SubEntity;  #--|                   |
            ex:pred3 ?obj3;    #  | star pattern      | graph star pattern
            ex:pred4 ?obj4.    #--|                   |
                               #                      |
        ?obj2 a ex:SubEntity2; #--|                   |
            ex:pred5 ?obj5;    #  | star pattern      |
            ex:pred6 ?obj6.    #--|-------------------|
    }
    ```
    </div>
- <dfn>normalized shape</dfn>: a representation of a SHACL node shape containing its identifier, open or closed status,
    positive and negative property constraints, object constraints, disjunctions, and references to other normalized shapes.
    A shape is closed exactly when its `sh:closed` value is `true`; otherwise it is open.
    A <dfn>negative property constraint</dfn> is a property constraint declared under `sh:not`. It states that conforming data
    must **not** carry the property, and is therefore the only constraint of an open shape that can positively establish
    incompatibility.
- <dfn>pattern-shape match</dfn>: a relation <math><mi>tp</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>c</mi></math> between a [=triple pattern=]
    <math><mi>tp</mi></math> and a normalized shape property constraint <math><mi>c</mi></math>. Their predicates must match.
    Explicit query object restrictions must satisfy the supported shape constraints, and retained query filters must not be
    provably contradictory. An object variable for which no applicable restriction is available is compatible rather than
    contradictory. A [=negative property constraint=] never yields a [=pattern-shape match=]: a triple pattern whose predicate
    the shape forbids is instead a <dfn>negative contradiction</dfn> for that shape.
    This is a conservative approximation of the paper's `match` relation, which requires the domain of triples
    matched by the query pattern to be a subset of the domain permitted by the shape pattern.
- <dfn export>star-shape subsumption</dfn>: a [=star pattern=] is subsumed by a normalized shape when every **required** triple
    pattern has a [=pattern-shape match=] in the shape body or in one accepted disjunctive branch, at least one triple pattern
    has such a match, and every [=star-pattern dependency=]
    is recursively subsumed by the referenced shape. Implementations must memoize visited star/shape pairs, or use an equivalent
    mechanism, so cyclic dependencies terminate.
    A triple pattern is required unless it originates from an `OPTIONAL` graph pattern or from a shape property constraint
    whose `sh:minCount` is zero. Optional triple patterns never prevent subsumption, because a resource can answer the query
    without them. This rule is the same for open and closed shapes.

The Boolean relation <math><mi>Q</mi><msub><mo>⊑</mo><mi>qs</mi></msub><mi>S</mi></math> from
[[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] establishes query relevance when at least one [=star pattern=] of at least one [=graph star pattern=]
of <math><mi>Q</mi></math>, taken as a root, is subsumed by the query obtained from shape <math><mi>S</mi></math>. This specification retains that local relation as the basis of a full
match, but extends its Boolean output with partial-evidence classifications. In particular, [=contained=] below is deliberately
stronger: it requires every normalized star pattern of the discovery input to be subsumed by at least one eligible resource shape.

This specification departs from [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] in two respects that an implementer familiar with that work should note.

**Open shapes are graded rather than treated as unconstrained.** [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] translates an open shape into a query
over the entire knowledge graph, on the grounds that such a shape imposes only the minimal constraints its data must satisfy;
subsumption by an open shape is consequently always satisfied. That treatment is appropriate for link pruning, where the only
question is whether a resource may be skipped. Discovery instead has to rank resources that cannot be excluded, so this
specification distinguishes degrees of partial evidence over open shapes: [=aligned=] when some of the input is positively
covered, and [=weakly rejected=] when none of it is but the shape does not exclude it either. The complete scale is defined in
[[#discovery-result]]. Only [=rejected=] carries the exclusion force of the Boolean relation.

**Resource shapes are not translated into queries.** [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] compares two queries, having obtained
<math><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mfenced><mi>S</mi></mfenced></math> from the shape. This specification compares a
query against shape constraints directly, as a [=normalized shape=] ([[#algorithm-steps]], step 2). The translation to a query
is applied only to an [=input shape=], so that a shape-based and a query-based discovery request are normalized to the same
representation. The comparison is otherwise the same relation, and the direction is fixed: the input is the source and the
resource shapes are the candidate targets.

## Logical steps ## {#algorithm-steps}

The algorithm accepts the following inputs:

1. exactly one of:
    - an <dfn>input shape</dfn>: the user-provided SHACL shape defining the discovery constraints; or
    - an <dfn>input query</dfn>: the user-provided SPARQL query defining the discovery constraints; and
2. one or more <dfn>resource shapes</dfn>: the [=available shapes=] of a candidate RDF [=resource=], that is, every SHACL shape
    extracted for it in [[#extract-shapes]]; and
3. the subset of those shapes that are [=deciding shapes=], as determined in [[#resource-relevance]]. When role-based filtering
    does not apply, every [=resource shape=] is a deciding shape.

When comparing shapes, the [=input shape=] is the source and the [=resource shapes=] are the candidate targets.
An implementation must not reverse this orientation: containment means that a target resource shape covers the constraints
expressed by the source input shape.

The algorithm proceeds as follows:

1. **Normalize the input constraints.**
    1. An [=input query=] is parsed and its [=triple patterns=] are grouped by subject into [=star patterns=].
        When the object of a triple pattern is also the subject of another star pattern, the latter is recorded as a dependency
        of the former. Supported `OPTIONAL`, `UNION`, `VALUES`, property-path, and `FILTER` forms are retained as described in
        [[#algorithm-profile]]; constructs outside that profile are discarded, as specified in [[#algorithm-profile]].
    2. An [=input shape=] is translated to the same query representation. Positive SHACL property constraints become triple
        patterns; `sh:node` relations become star-pattern dependencies; `sh:or` and `sh:xone` become alternative branches; and
        supported datatype, numeric-bound, and regular-expression facets become filter constraints.
    3. A triple pattern whose predicate is a variable is an <dfn>unrestricted triple pattern</dfn>. It yields no
        [=star pattern=], because a [=shape=] constrains named predicates, but it asks for every triple of a resource.
        An input whose triple patterns are all unrestricted is therefore [=contained=] by any [=resource shape=], and every
        resource shape must be marked visitable: the input is maximally unselective, which is a property of the input and not
        a failure to assess it.
    4. Normalization must otherwise yield at least one [=star pattern=]. An input that yields none and contains no
        [=unrestricted triple pattern=] never asks the resource for data — its answer is the same whichever resource is
        considered — so relevance is undefined and a client must not assign it a [=discovery result=]. An empty `WHERE`
        clause, a body of only `VALUES` or `FILTER`, and a body left empty after discarding out-of-profile constructs
        ([[#out-of-profile]]) are of this kind.
2. **Normalize the resource shapes.** Each [=resource shape=] becomes a [=normalized shape=] represented by its IRI, open or closed status,
    property constraints, cardinalities, value constraints, [=negative property constraints=], disjunctions, and references to
    other shapes. A shape is closed when `sh:closed` is `true`; otherwise it is open.
3. **Bind star patterns to resource shapes.** For every normalized star pattern and every resource shape, the algorithm:
    1. computes [=pattern-shape matches=] for predicates and supported object constraints;
    2. checks compatible datatype, class, numeric-bound, and regular-expression constraints;
    3. recursively evaluates dependent star patterns against referenced shapes, while tracking visited dependencies to terminate cycles.
          A [=star-pattern dependency=] that is not established is <dfn>refuted</dfn> only when the referenced shape leaves a
          required nested triple pattern unmatched while being closed, or yields a [=negative contradiction=]. When the
          referenced shape is open and merely does not describe the nested pattern, the dependency is undecided rather than
          refuted: the predicate of the referring triple pattern still matched the resource shape, which step 4 counts as a
          partial match. A resource shape reached only through `sh:node` also governs the nested star pattern it resolves, and
          that pattern must be classified against it in step 4, whether or not the shape is itself a [=deciding shape=];
    4. evaluates `UNION` or SHACL disjunction branches; and
    5. checks whether retained filters contradict the resource shape constraints. A filter that cannot be decided from shape
          information alone must not cause rejection; a provable contradiction prevents that shape from contributing a match; and
    6. checks for a [=negative contradiction=]. A resource shape that forbids the predicate of a required triple pattern must
          not contribute a match, and must not be counted as an open shape in step 4, because it establishes incompatibility
          rather than remaining silent about the property.
4. **Classify each star pattern.** Considering only the [=deciding shapes=] of the candidate resource, assign the first
     applicable result in the following order. A non-deciding [=resource shape=] takes part in step 3, so that
     [=star-pattern dependencies=] resolve, but never contributes to this step.
    1. [=contained=], if the root star pattern and all its required nested patterns are subsumed. A single deciding shape is
        normally responsible for this, but a disjunctive branch left unresolved by that shape may be subsumed by another
        deciding shape of the same candidate resource;
    2. [=aligned=], if at least one triple in the root star pattern binds to an open resource shape;
    3. [=unaligned=], if a root triple binds only to a closed resource shape, or if only a nested star pattern binds. A root
        triple whose predicate matched a closed resource shape but whose [=star-pattern dependency=] was undecided rather than
        [=refuted=] counts as binding for this purpose;
    4. [=weakly rejected=], if no triple binds and at least one deciding shape is open; or
    5. [=rejected=], if no triple binds and every resource shape that describes the star pattern is closed or yields a
        [=negative contradiction=], or the resource has no deciding shape at all. A star pattern must not be [=rejected=]
        on the strength of shapes that never described it.
5. **Aggregate the result.** The result for the complete input is the least favorable result assigned to any of its star patterns,
    according to the ordering defined in [[#discovery-result]]. Consequently, the complete input is [=contained=] only when every
    star pattern is [=contained=].

The algorithm returns a [=discovery result=] containing the aggregate result, the result and matching shape IRIs for each
star pattern, the corresponding predicate-level bindings, and a visit indication for every resource shape. A resource shape
is marked as visitable when at least one input triple pattern binds to it. This indication is independent from the aggregate
result and may be used by a client to decide whether to retrieve data associated with the shape.

## Supported constraint profile ## {#algorithm-profile}

Conforming implementations must document the SPARQL and SHACL fragment they evaluate. The reference implementation supports:

- SPARQL basic triple patterns with IRI predicates; `OPTIONAL`; `UNION`; object-variable substitution from `VALUES`;
    alternative paths; negated property sets; and the `?`, `*`, and `+` path cardinality forms over a single IRI path.
    Sequence and inverse paths are partially normalized when they occur as branches of an alternative path;
- conservative `FILTER` compatibility for equality and inequality comparisons, numeric bounds, conjunction, disjunction,
    and regular expressions; an undecidable expression produces unknown evidence and must not produce rejection; and
- SHACL property paths represented by IRIs, `sh:closed`, `sh:node`, `sh:class`, `sh:datatype`, `sh:or`, `sh:xone`,
    numeric bounds, `sh:pattern` with optional flags, and `sh:not` over a property shape, which yields a
    [=negative property constraint=].

The reference implementation records path and SHACL cardinalities, and treats a minimum cardinality of zero as optional,
but does not otherwise compare cardinality ranges during binding. `sh:not` over anything other than a property shape,
top-level sequence or inverse paths, and arbitrarily nested property paths are outside the supported profile.

Note: This specification does not define the use of ShEx [[SHEX-PRIMER]] to describe resource content. The reference implementation
additionally accepts ShEx shapes and normalizes them to the same [=normalized shape=] representation, but only the SHACL
fragment listed above is in scope here.

### Handling constructs outside the profile ### {#out-of-profile}

A construct outside the supported profile must be **discarded** during normalization: it must not contribute [=triple patterns=]
to the normalized input. In particular, a client must discard `MINUS` and `FILTER NOT EXISTS` graph patterns, `SERVICE`
clauses, sub-`SELECT`s and aggregates, and `BIND`.

Note: An [=unrestricted triple pattern=] is not a discarded construct. It contributes no [=star pattern=], but it does express
a constraint — that the resource holds triples — which every resource satisfies. See [[#algorithm-steps]].

Discarding a construct removes constraints from the input, so the resulting [=discovery result=] is computed over a sub-input
of the one given. This can only over-estimate relevance; it can never cause a relevant resource to be classified lower than it
would otherwise be, and therefore preserves the guarantee stated in [[#discovery-result]]. Retaining such a construct as an
ordinary conjunctive pattern must be avoided, because `MINUS` and `SERVICE` patterns describe, respectively, solutions to
exclude and data held elsewhere; treating them as required patterns changes the result in both directions.

A client should report which constructs it discarded, so that a consumer can tell that the result approximates the input.
If discarding leaves no [=star pattern=] at all, the rule in [[#algorithm-steps]] applies and no [=discovery result=] is
produced.


# Discovery result # {#discovery-result}

A <dfn id="discovery-result-definition">discovery result</dfn> expresses the degree to which a candidate resource's shapes satisfy the input constraints.
The following result values are ordered from highest to lowest relevance:

<table class="def">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Result</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>contained</dfn> (<code>CONTAINED</code>)</td>
            <td>Every input star pattern, including its required nested patterns, is fully covered by the resource shapes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>aligned</dfn> (<code>ALIGNED</code>)</td>
            <td>The input is not contained, but at least one root triple pattern matches an open resource shape. Because the shape is open, unmatched properties do not establish incompatibility.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>unaligned</dfn> (<code>UNALIGNED</code>)</td>
            <td>The input is neither contained nor aligned, but a root triple pattern matches a closed resource shape, or a match exists only in a nested star pattern. A predicate that a resource shape declares but whose [=star-pattern dependency=] was undecided rather than [=refuted=] counts as such a match.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>weakly rejected</dfn> (<code>WEAKLY_REJECTED</code>)</td>
            <td>No triple pattern matches, but at least one resource shape is open. The absence of a declared property is therefore not definitive evidence of incompatibility.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>rejected</dfn> (<code>REJECTED</code>)</td>
            <td>No triple pattern matches and every resource shape that describes the star pattern is closed or forbids a required predicate (a [=negative contradiction=]), or no eligible resource shape is available.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

The relevance ordering is:

<p class="equation"><code>CONTAINED &gt; ALIGNED &gt; UNALIGNED &gt; WEAKLY_REJECTED &gt; REJECTED</code>.</p>

For an input containing multiple star patterns, the aggregate result must be the lowest result in this ordering among all
per-star-pattern results. A discovery client should order returned resources by this aggregate result. Resources with equal
aggregate results may be ordered using application-specific criteria. A client may omit [=rejected=] resources from the returned
subset, but must preserve their classification when they are included. Omitting them does not discard a resource that could have
satisfied the input constraints: [=rejected=] is assigned only when every [=resource shape=] that describes the star pattern is
closed or yields a [=negative contradiction=], and in both cases the shape positively establishes that the required properties
are absent. An open shape reached only through `sh:node` therefore prevents [=rejected=] just as a candidate open shape does,
because it forbids nothing.
A client must not omit resources classified [=weakly rejected=] on the same grounds, because an open shape that simply does not
declare a property is not evidence that the property is absent.

The per-star-pattern details and visit indications are supporting evidence rather than additional relevance degrees. In particular,
a resource shape may be marked as visitable after a partial binding even when the resource's aggregate result is [=unaligned=].

A [=discovery result=] computed from an input from which constructs were discarded ([[#out-of-profile]]) approximates a sub-input
of the one given, and should be reported together with the list of discarded constructs.

Note: The reference implementation currently exposes the misspelled identifier <code>UNALINGED</code> for
<code>UNALIGNED</code>. These identifiers denote the same result; serializations defined by this specification should use
<code>UNALIGNED</code>.

